<p>
For most websites, WebP provides the best balance between compatibility and
performance, while AVIF offers maximum compression benefits.
</p>

<h2>Responsive Images</h2>

<p>
Different devices require different image sizes. A large desktop image should
not be delivered to a small mobile screen when a smaller version would provide
the same visual experience.
</p>

<p>
Responsive images allow browsers to choose the most appropriate image size
based on device capabilities.
</p>

<pre>
&lt;img
src="image-1200.webp"
srcset="
image-400.webp 400w,
image-800.webp 800w,
image-1200.webp 1200w"
sizes="100vw"
alt="Responsive example"&gt;
</pre>

<p>
This technique reduces bandwidth usage and improves loading performance.
</p>

<h2>Lazy Loading</h2>

<p>
Not every image needs to load immediately. Images positioned far below the
visible area of a page can be loaded only when users approach them.
</p>

<p>
This strategy is known as lazy loading.
</p>

<pre>
&lt;img
src="example.webp"
loading="lazy"
alt="Example image"&gt;
</pre>

<p>
Lazy loading reduces initial page weight and helps users access important
content more quickly.
</p>

<p>
However, important above-the-fold images should generally not be lazy loaded
because doing so may delay rendering.
</p>

<h2>Image SEO and Core Web Vitals</h2>

<p>
Image optimization directly affects several Core Web Vitals metrics.
</p>

<p>
The most important metric influenced by images is Largest Contentful Paint
(LCP), which measures how quickly the largest visible content element appears.
</p>

<p>
Large hero images often become the LCP element on a page.
</p>

<p>
When these images are compressed and delivered efficiently, loading speed
improves significantly.
</p>

<p>
For a deeper explanation, visit our guide:
</p>

